Design for M2.4-tail + M2.5 (ADR-037):

Revocation marking (M2.4): in worker + drain send loops: when a Gmail leg throws code === 'gmail_revoked', mark that project's gmail transport status = 'revoked' (idempotent UPDATE ... WHERE status != 'revoked'), write audit_logs entry transport.gmail_revoked, then fall through to the NEXT chain leg (SES) instead of hard-failing the email — because revoked is a transport health issue, not message-invalid; SES leg keeps the mail flowing (failover philosophy from DESIGN). Careful: with only-gmail chain, job fails → terminal (email marked failed? current logic: non-transient thrown from the last leg → job fails permanently). New behavior: revoked → mark transport, try next leg; if no more legs, throw → failed. Where in drain: catch inside for-loop — if (code === 'gmail_revoked') { markRevoked(); continue; }. Worker same. buildGmail instantiates a NEW GmailTransport per drain so status flip prevents future construction? Chain construction reads def.status === 'active' → revoked row skipped ✓ (status filter exists at resolveChain!).

Abuse watch (M2.5): Pure decision engine packages/db/src/gmail-abuse.ts (or apps/api/src/lib/gmail-watch.ts — must be usable by worker too → put in @calder/db beside usage.ts, since both apps depend on db):

assessGmailVelocity(db, { projectId, transportId }) → reads gmail sends in windows: last hour count (velPerHour), last 24h count, prior 7-day daily average baseline (from emails where transport='gmail'). Tiered verdict:

    ok
    warn: vel > GMAIL_VELOCITY_WARN_PER_HOUR (e.g. 150/hr) or today's count > 60% of cap → level 'warn'
    limit: vel > warn*2 or cap exhausted repeatedly → status stays 'active' but throttle? 'limit' action = hard-stop further gmail sends this hour (throw gmail_limited transient → retry next hour)
    suspend: sustained >2 days over cap or absurd burst (>1k/hr) → transport status → 'suspended' + audit log + (appeal: founder can re-activate via control).

Env-configurable thresholds in @calder/config: GMAIL_VELOCITY_*. Decision function pure & unit-testable (given counts → verdict), plus a thin enforceGmailVelocity(db, projectId) called in buildGmail before constructing transport (drain + worker): throws ProviderError-like { code: 'gmail_velocity_limit', transient: true/false } → for 'limit' transient=true so chain failover/retry handles; for 'suspend' transient=false+ status suspended + audit row ONCE (idempotency: only write audit if status actually transitioned).*_

Audit entries: actions transport.gmail_velocity_warn (debounced: only if no warn in last 24h), transport.gmail_suspended, transport.gmail_revoked, metadata {projectId, transportId, counts, cap}.

Control inventory: apps/dashboard/app/control/customers/transports/page.tsx? Check control nav groups to add a link — but keep UI minimal: a server-rendered list of ALL project_transports joined org+project with gmail type: columns org/project/label/status/lastUsed/day-count. Route: /control/customers/transports + sidebarNav entry. Backend query helper in lib/control/queries.ts (follow conventions there).

Graduation prompts (M2.4): in dashboard senders page (app/(app)/senders/page.tsx) — if org has gmail transport AND its 7-day average usage > threshold (e.g. 50/day) OR cap-hit in last 3 days → banner panel "You've outgrown Gmail — connect your AWS SES (or use Calder managed)". Backend helper gmailGraduationSignal(db, projectIds) in @calder/db. SSR page — just render a notice div (uses existing styles).

