Reading the exact H4/code surfaces to fix:

Key insight on H4 (oauth.ts:179-184 area): the branch STILL links when !profile.emailVerified && same[0].emailVerifiedAt != null — i.e. if the Calder account already has a verified email, an attacker with an UNVERIFIED provider-side email control could link. That's the takeover primitive: attacker registers gmail address victim-typo@… (no — they'd need control of victim's email at the provider... The scenario: OAuth provider returns an email that the attacker controls at provider level but does NOT own — e.g., GitHub unverified email set to victim@target.com. If Calder sees existing verified user victim@target.com and profile.emailVerified=false → current code links! → attacker signs in as victim.)

The correct H4 decision per roadmap: "links on attacker-controllable unverified provider email? Confirm guarantees first." → DECISION: require provider-verified email for auto-link, double-verify. New rule: if profile.emailVerified is false → never auto-link (throw with sign-in-first message) regardless of the existing row's verification state. Google: email_verified claim ✓. GitHub: arctic's GitHub fetch of /user gives email but not verified; verified status comes from /user/emails entries. Check fetchGithubProfile:

GitHub fetches verified status properly; Google uses email_verified. H4 fix = tighten the link rule:
ts

if (same[0]) {
  if (!profile.emailVerified) {
    // H4 decision (ADR-040): an unverified-at-provider email NEVER links to
    // an existing Calder account, verified or not. Sign in with the original
    // method and link OAuth from settings once both sides agree.
    throw new Error("That email is already registered and this provider hasn't verified it. Sign in with your original method to link accounts.");
  }
  userId = same[0].id;
}

Bonus: when profile.emailVerified=true and linking to existing account, also mark Calder email verified if not yet (safe — provider verified it).

