  (If the name isn't obvious, ask the user for the exact branch name before
   continuing. Do not guess.)

STEP 1 — materialize + verify (decisive, no halfway)
  git fetch origin transfer-tmp
  git show origin/transfer-tmp:calder.patch > calder.patch
  wc -c calder.patch        # MUST be exactly 1268023
  echo "fea32f1b618186a77d3c266c170d7ecf6a157526604733f21a50cd29c22b7947  calder.patch" | sha256sum -c -
  # MUST print "calder.patch: OK". Any mismatch → STOP and tell the user;
  # the upload was truncated/corrupted and must be redone by the human.
  # If git-show fails because the upload used a different filename, list
  # branch contents first: git ls-tree --name-only origin/transfer-tmp

STEP 2 — apply
  git am --3way < calder.patch
  # If any conflict is reported: STOP. Do NOT resolve silently. Reply to the
  # user with the exact list of conflicted files — resolutions come from the
  # session that authored the patch (its tree still exists).

STEP 3 — gates, in this order (build BEFORE test; a previous failure here
was just @calder/db not being built when tests ran):
  corepack pnpm install
  corepack pnpm -r --filter './packages/*' run build
  corepack pnpm -r --if-present test
  # expect ~242 passed / 0 failed across packages incl. new packages/sdk-node
  (cd apps/dashboard && corepack pnpm run build)
